cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "cielociego: how often an optical satellite can actually see a field"
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "del Rio Mejia"
    given-names: "Jhonatan"
    alias: EazyHood
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22132250"
repository-code: "https://github.com/EazyHood/cielociego"
url: "https://eazyhood.github.io/cielociego/"
abstract: >-
  Measures, over a field's real polygon rather than over the whole 110 km tile,
  how often an optical satellite can actually see it, by reading the Sentinel-2
  Scene Classification Layer inside the boundary instead of trusting the
  scene-level eo:cloud_cover metadata. Measured over two fields in Magdalena,
  Colombia across 2,794 days (2019-2026): Sentinel-2 acquired imagery on 599 and
  601 of those days, and cloud measured inside the polygon made 47 % and 56 % of
  those acquisitions unusable. No usable view existed on 89 % and 91 % of days,
  of which about 78.5 percentage points are the orbital revisit and 10 to 12
  points are cloud. Uses Sentinel-2 L2A, Sentinel-1 GRD and Sentinel-1 RTC from
  public catalogues, with no account and no cost.
keywords:
  - teledeteccion
  - remote-sensing
  - sentinel-2
  - sentinel-1
  - sar
  - cloud-cover
  - tropical-agriculture
  - colombia
